<p>&lt;Click to Enlarge&gt; With her children almost full grown, she turned to civic affairs. At the time, she was the oldest woman in the state to hold an elected office as a member of the Mandan Park Board. She served ten years helped to establish several parks including the former Civic Memorial Park (location of the current Collins Avenue Post Office.) As President of the Mandan Flower Club, she was instrumential in getting the gladiola, her favorite flower, as the City's official flower.</p>

<p>With her focus on city beautification, she helped to organize the Civic Improvement League, serving as its President for the first 23 years of its existence and subsequent multiple terms on its Board of Directors. She presided over the ND Humane Society. Mrs Stark also served as President of the Mandan chapter of the War Mothers and was active in their State Chapter including serving in several officer roles. She was also Past Worthy Matron of the Eastern Star. Anna Stark died on October 6, 1944 at 94 and is buried in Union Cemetery beside her husband, daughter Mary, son George Jr. and his wife.</p>
